# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the Furrowe parlour, the Friday morning
@node e1.bet.parlour
place: q1_furrowe
hud-clock: 08:30
speaker: BET
cost: 0
nar: The Furrowe parlour, the morning after the loft. A good room nobody sits in: a horsehair sofa, a clock under a glass dome, a brass scale weight from the old exchange on the mantel, polished, that Abel was given when he made inspector. The fire's laid and not lit.
nar: Bet Carrow has come in by the back, through the scullery, because that's the way she's always come into this house, since she was fifteen and did the Furrowes' step on a Saturday for sixpence. She has Anni on her hip. She hasn't been asked to sit and she doesn't.
nar: Abel is at the table by the window with an envelope in front of him, square to the edge, and beside it a big iron key on a loop of string, the key to the end barn at Haldane's, that's hung on his back door since Purkiss gave it up.
line: ABEL: He's said yes.
line: BET: I know. He came by ours last night with his arm in a sack and asked my dad for me. My dad laughed at him for a quarter of an hour.
line: ABEL: What's wrong with his arm.
line: BET: You'd have to ask Josh Brack's dog. {when: !has(e1.dog_called)}
line: BET: He says a hawthorn. He says it like he's proud of it. {when: has(e1.dog_called)}
nar: Abel looks at the envelope for a while.
line: ABEL: The barns at Haldane's, then. The house and the end barn and the paddock. At Purkiss's rent, which was fair, and which I've not put up.
line: BET: Purkiss paid four and six.
line: ABEL: Purkiss paid four and six.
line: BET: And what else.
line: ABEL: There's twelve pound in that for the wedding and the setting up. You'll not need to say where it's from.
line: BET: Who'd I say it to.
line: ABEL: And the little one's his. As far as anybody asks. That's a kindness to her, Bet. You know it is. A child wants a name she can say.
nar: Through the wall, in the back room, Mary Furrowe's baby starts crying, the thin new crying of a baby a week old. Then Mary's voice, low, singing to it. Bet turns her head to the wall and listens to it. Anni turns her head too, because her mother did.
line: BET: That's Pell.
line: ABEL: That's Pell.
line: BET: He's got a good pair of lungs.
line: ABEL: He has.
nar: Bet hitches Anni up her hip. Anni has got hold of Bet's collar and is chewing it.
line: BET: She'll never come to this house. Front or back. That's what you're paying for. I did your step for five years, Mr Furrowe. I know what a back door's for.
line: ABEL: I'd not put it like that.
line: BET: You'd never put it any way at all. That's what the twelve pound's for.
line: BET: And Ned's not to come to the barns. Not for the rent. Not for anything. You'll send somebody.
line: ABEL: I'll come for the rent myself.
line: BET: Then you'll come. Not him.
line: ABEL: Not him.
nar: She takes the envelope off the table and doesn't open it to count it. She puts it in the front of her dress.
alt: e1.bet.rent ? q_class == inspector
go: e1.bet.door

@node e1.bet.rent
place: q1_furrowe
speaker: BET
cost: 0
line: BET: One more thing. The four and six. I want you to say it stays four and six, whoever has the barns after you, as long as I'm in them. And I want you to say it at the wedding, out loud, in the barns, in front of my dad and Edie and every hand that's left. Not in here.
voice: sum: Four and six for the rest of her life. The barns'll be worth twice that by the time she's grey. {dc: 2}
voice: pride: She's a Carrow off the Osby road. She wants you stood up in a barn promising her things. {dc: 2}
opt: "Say it: I'll come and say it. In front of whoever you like." -> e1.bet.door {do: set(e1.rent_fixed)}
opt: "Say it: The rent's the rent, Bet. It'll be fair. You've my word on it, here." -> e1.bet.door {do: set(e1.rent_open)}

@node e1.bet.door
place: q1_furrowe
speaker: BET
cost: 0
line: ABEL: I'll say it. Sunday. {when: has(e1.rent_fixed)}
line: BET: Your word. Right. In here. {when: has(e1.rent_open)}
nar: She goes out by the scullery the way she came. In the passage she stops by the back room door, where the singing is, and stands there with Anni for as long as it takes to say a short prayer, and then goes on.
nar: Abel sits at the table and picks up the barn key by its string and weighs it in his hand. Purkiss's key. He hangs it on his finger and lets it turn. It isn't anybody's yet. It won't be till Friday, when the hands are out of the barn.
go: e1.bet.edie

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- what Bet knows, told to Edie at the fitting
@node e1.bet.edie
place: q1_edie
speaker: EDIE
cost: 0
nar: Edie's room over the draper's, the same afternoon. A dressmaker's dummy with no head, pins in a tin, a long glass with a crack across one corner. Bet is standing on a chair in her good blue, the one Edie let out in the spring of the year before last, being taken back in.
line: EDIE: Breathe in.
line: BET: I am breathing in.
line: EDIE: Breathe in like you mean it. You've been eating Furrowe's bread.
line: BET: I've been eating my dad's bread. It's all chaff.
nar: Edie has pins in her mouth and talks round them. She takes the waist in an inch, and another inch, kneeling on the floor with the hem across her knees. Anni is asleep on Edie's bed on a pile of offcuts.
line: EDIE: So it's the hill man.
line: BET: It's Ilo.
line: EDIE: It's Ilo who sings on the rick with his shirt off.
line: BET: That's him.
line: EDIE: Does he know.
line: BET: Know what.
nar: Edie takes the pins out of her mouth one at a time and puts them in the cushion.
line: EDIE: Bet. I let this out in March. I let it out again in May. I know what I let it out for.
nar: Bet looks at herself in the cracked glass for a long time, in the blue dress, standing on a chair, like a statue of somebody.
line: BET: He knows. He was at the wedding. Ned's. He carried the chairs.
line: EDIE: And he's having you anyway.
line: BET: He's having a roof. Furrowe's giving him the barns and a name, and he stops down here when the frost comes. I'm in with the barns. Like the door.
line: EDIE: That's a horrible thing to say.
line: BET: It's what it is, though. He's the least daft man I know. He asked my dad for me with his arm bleeding through a sack, and my dad laughed at him, and he stood there and let him.
nar: Edie gets up off the floor and stands back to look at the waist. It's good. It's the best she's done.
line: EDIE: Do you like him.
line: BET: I like him better than Ned. Ned cried.
line: EDIE: When.
line: BET: When I told him. He sat on my dad's wall and cried, and I had to tell him it'd be all right. Me. Telling him.
nar: She gets down off the chair, careful of the pins.
line: BET: You'll not say. To anybody. Not your mam, not Will Brack.
line: EDIE: Who'd I say it to.
line: BET: That's what I said to Furrowe.
go: e1.supper

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the wedding, the Sunday
@node e1.wedding
place: q1_barns
speaker: CARROW
cost: 0
nar: The Sunday. The wedding is at nine at the Furrow Church, before the service, so the vicar can do it and then do the service, and there are six people at it, and one of them is asleep, and that's Anni.
nar: After, in the barns at Haldane's, the hands who are left have made a table out of planks on two barrels, and there's a ham end from Orme's supper that Edie got from the barn the morning after, and bread, and a cake Bet's mother made with no eggs in it that sinks in the middle like a saddle.
nar: Old Carrow has his fiddle, which is worse than Orme's fiddler's but knows more than four tunes. He plays sitting on a cask with his eyes shut. Ilo dances with Bet in the straw, badly, with his bad arm held up out of the way like a man carrying a tray.
line: CARROW: You dance like a hen in a wind.
line: ILO: I dance like a hill man.
line: CARROW: That's what I said.
nar: Then Ilo dances with Anni, holding her up, swinging her round till she screams, and puts her down dizzy, and she falls on her bottom in the straw and looks surprised, and gets up and holds her arms up for more.
nar: Halfway through, Abel Furrowe comes into the barns in his good hat, and the fiddle stops. He stands on the step of the cart with everybody looking at him and says it: the barns at four and six, as long as Bet's in them, whoever has them after him. Old Carrow spits in his palm and holds it out, and Abel looks at it, and spits in his own and shakes it, and the hands bang the planks. Bet doesn't bang anything. She watches his face the whole time he says it. {when: has(e1.rent_fixed)}
nar: Bet's mother takes Ilo by the face with both hands in the yard and looks at him a long time, the way you'd look at a horse you'd been sold, and then kisses him on the forehead, hard, and lets go.
go: e1.osby
